## Summary

Introduces **widgets** — a lightweight pattern for embedding self-contained UI components on a page without CMS-authored block content.

Think of widgets as **blocks without content**. Blocks are ideal when authors structure content in the document (headings, images, links, rows/columns). Widgets are better when the page only needs a placeholder and configuration — search boxes, login forms, calculators, maps, chat embeds, or any component whose markup lives in code rather than in authored tables.

Authors reference a widget with a single link:

```markdown
[Site Search](/widgets/search/site-search.html?q=docs)
```

Auto-blocking turns that link into a `widget` block, which fetches and mounts the widget's own HTML, CSS, and JS from `/widgets/` in the repository.

## Widgets vs blocks

| | **Block** | **Widget** |
|---|-----------|------------|
| **Content** | Authored in the document (tables, rows, cells) | None — only a link reference |
| **Markup** | Built by decorating authored HTML | Fetched from `/widgets/{path}/{name}.html` |
| **Styles / behavior** | `blocks/{name}/{name}.css/js` | `widgets/{path}/{name}.css/js` |
| **Configuration** | Block structure + metadata | URL query parameters → `data-*` attributes |
| **Good for** | Content-driven layouts (cards, columns, hero) | Code-driven components (forms, widgets, embeds) |

If you find yourself building a block with lots of empty placeholders and no real authored content — just configuration — it is likely a widget.

## Authoring

Any link containing `/widgets/` is auto-converted during page decoration:

```
/widgets/name.html
/widgets/path1/name.html
/widgets/path1/path2/name.html?theme=dark&foo=bar
```

Intermediate path segments locate assets on disk. The **filename** (without extension) drives CSS class names in the DOM.

Query parameters are copied onto the widget root as `data-*` attributes, so authors can pass simple configuration without CMS block structure.

## Widget project structure

Each widget is a small, self-contained bundle under `/widgets/`:

```
widgets/
  search/
    site-search.html    # boilerplate HTML inserted into the DOM
    site-search.css     # widget-scoped styles
    site-search.js      # export default async function decorate(el) { … }
    site-search.json    # optional: string literals for translation
```

The `.html` file is the widget's initial DOM. The `.js` file follows the same decorate pattern as blocks. A `.json` alongside is a common place for i18n strings that need translation.

## Resulting DOM

For `/widgets/path1/path2/name.html`, after decoration:

```html
<div class="section name-container">
  <div class="name-wrapper">
    <div class="widget name"
         data-block-name="widget"
         data-block-status="loaded"
         data-source="/widgets/path1/path2/name.html">
      <!-- fetched widget HTML -->
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```

Generic block scaffold classes (`block`, `widget-wrapper`, `widget-container`) are removed after load. The widget filename becomes the CSS scope (`name`, `name-wrapper`, `name-container`). Widget-specific styles belong in `/widgets/…/name.css`, not in `blocks/widget/widget.css` (which only covers the loader).

## Implementation

- `scripts/scripts.js` — `buildWidgetAutoBlocks()` converts `/widgets/` links into widget blocks (same phase as fragment auto-loading and hero auto-blocking)
- `blocks/widget/widget.js` — parses the href, fetches HTML/CSS/JS, applies classes and data attributes, runs the widget's decorate function
- `blocks/widget/widget.css` — minimal block-loader styles to satisfy the block CSS pipeline

